  "title": "Three Days of Fire",
  "subtitle": "Earthquake, Fire, and the Forging of Modern San Francisco",
  "authorDisplayName": "Liszt Think",
  "description": "At 5:12 a.m. on April 18, 1906, San Francisco shattered. The true catastrophe began moments later when Fire Chief Dennis Sullivan died in the rubble and water mains ruptured, leaving the city defenseless against spreading flames. Over three days, General Frederick Funston’s troops enforced Mayor Eugene Schmitz’s shoot-to-kill order while dynamiting created firebreaks that often fed the inferno consuming Chinatown and Nob Hill. Drawing on army reports, relief ledgers, and survivor letters, this narrative reconstructs the disaster through conflicting official records and firsthand accounts. It traces the chaotic military response, the refugee camps in Golden Gate Park, and the deliberate political decision to rebrand the catastrophe as a fire rather than an earthquake. From the disputed death toll to the unequal rebuilding that followed, every claim is anchored in verifiable evidence. This is not a myth of resilience but a documented reckoning with how a metropolis burned, who survived, and what was sacrificed to raise a new city upon the ashes.",
